Day 17 - AWS CloudFront Practical

1. CloudFront Distribution

Today I created and practiced an AWS CloudFront distribution to deliver my S3 static website through a CDN.

2. CloudFront Configuration

- Created a CloudFront distribution
- Connected CloudFront with my S3 website
- Practiced CloudFront distribution settings
- Enabled the distribution after configuration
- Checked the distribution status from the CloudFront console

3. Default Root Object

I learned that the default root object tells CloudFront which file to serve when a user opens the distribution without specifying a file.

For my static website, the default root object is:

index.html

The file name is case-sensitive.

4. CloudFront and S3

The basic flow I practiced was:

User
  ↓
CloudFront
  ↓
Edge Location
  ↓
S3
  ↓
Website Files

S3 stores the original website files, while CloudFront helps deliver the content to users through its CDN network.

5. Practical Troubleshooting

During the practical, the CloudFront website initially had a timeout issue.

I checked the CloudFront distribution and learned that the distribution must be enabled and correctly configured before testing the website.

I also learned the importance of checking the CloudFront origin configuration and default root object when troubleshooting website access.

6. What I Learned

- CloudFront distribution
- CloudFront and S3 connection
- Distribution status
- Enabling a CloudFront distribution
- Default root object
- "index.html"
- Basic CloudFront troubleshooting
- CloudFront request flow

Quick Revision

CloudFront is AWS's CDN service.

S3 stores the website files, and CloudFront delivers those files through its distributed network.

S3 = Stores content
CloudFront = Delivers content
Edge Location = Helps serve content closer to users

